723 0 obj
<<
/Title (A tuple containing only hashable immutable values can be a dictionary key because its hash remains stable. A list is mutable and unhashable, so using [13.0418, 80.2341] as a key raises TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 683 0 R
/Next 735 0 R
>>
endobj
724 0 obj
<<
/Title (A shallow copy is enough when the contents are immutable or when sharing nested objects is intended. Deep copy is needed when independent nested mutable objects are required. A function receives a reference to the same list object, so mutating it changes what the caller sees.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 683 0 R
/Prev 735 0 R
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Title (sys.getsizeof reports the container's shallow size, not the memory of all referenced integer objects. Both tuple and list indexing are O\(1\). Actual creation and size results depend on the Python build and platform.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 684 0 R
>>
endobj
726 0 obj
<<
/Title (Expected output: [\('seeds', 3\), \('price', 2\), \('received', 2\)]. Price comes before received because counts tie at 2 and the secondary sort key is alphabetical.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 686 0 R
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Group by district and crop; use get and setdefault)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 620 0 R
