/Last 739 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 689 0 R
>>
endobj
731 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 689 0 R
>>
endobj
732 0 obj
<<
/Title (General form: [expression for item in iterable if condition]. A comprehension becomes harder to read when it contains multiple nested loops, several conditions or complex transformations; a normal loop is clearer then.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 691 0 R
/Next 733 0 R
>>
endobj
733 0 obj
<<
/Title (A list comprehension immediately builds and stores a list \(O\(n\) memory\). A generator expression yields values on demand and usually uses O\(1\) extra iteration memory, though it can only be consumed once.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 691 0 R
/Prev 732 0 R
>>
endobj
734 0 obj
<<
/Title (All three methods should return identical valid readings. Test all-valid input such as [3, 4, 5], and all-invalid input such as [55, -55]; both should be checked explicitly. Timing is machine-dependent.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 692 0 R
>>
endobj
735 0 obj
<<
/Title (Memory concept: bad = [[0]*3]*3 creates one inner list referenced by all three outer positions. The corrected comprehension creates three distinct inner lists. In a shallow copy, the outer container is new but nested objects are shared. A deep copy recursively copies nested objects.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 683 0 R
/Prev 723 0 R
/Next 724 0 R
>>
endobj
736 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 727 0 R
/Next 737 0 R
>>
endobj
737 0 obj
<<
/Title (Python 3.7+ guarantees insertion order for dictionary keys. keys\(\), values\(\) and items\(\) return dynamic view objects reflecting the dictionary; items\(\) yields key-value pairs.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 727 0 R
/Prev 736 0 R
>>
endobj
738 0 obj
<<
/Title (Venn interpretation: intersection is the overlap; symmetric difference is the two non-overlapping regions; neither is the part of the universal student set outside the union. Sets remove duplicates automatically and do not preserve input order.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 730 0 R
/Next 739 0 R
>>
endobj
739 0 obj
<<
/Title (Set membership is O\(1\) average because of hashing; list membership is O\(n\) because items are checked sequentially. Lists cannot be set members because they are mutable/unhashable; tuples can be members when all their contents are hashable.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 730 0 R
/Prev 738 0 R
>>
endobj
